 hasn't prompted new  evacuations lava has destroyed over 1100  buildings on the island and displaced  about 6 000 people  the murder of a young sick man in nova  scotia last month continues to send  shock waves through the indian canadian  community there  the killing happened in the town of  truro northeast of halifax police have  provided few details about what led to  the attacks leaving some sikhs fearing  the killing was motivated by hate  ross lord reports  prabhjot singh khatri's father died when  he was just six months old he grew up  determined to help support his single  mother working two jobs in true nova  scotia in a fast food restaurant and as  a taxi driver after coming to canada  from india in 2017. he was just very  happy and working um  hard to send money to his mom in india  so she can have a comfortable life which  makes what happened to prabjot singh all  the more shocking  in the early morning hours of september  5th as he left his friend's apartment he  was attacked somebody stabbed him and he  ran upstairs he had enough energy to run  upstairs but then he fell and he died  inside the apartment  friends say he wasn't robbed there was  no obvious motive leading many six to  wonder if his killing was a hate crime  data shows more six have moved to  canadian towns in addition to big cities  leaders in the sikh community say  newcomers are sometimes vulnerable to  hostility and violence there's a lot of  suspicion that  what drove this murder was intolerance  and racism in a statement the high  commission of india said we urge the  canadian government to ensure the safety  of all indian nationals especially the  indian students in canada from racially  motivated crimes the high commission  advised all indian citizens in canada  particularly students to exercise  caution  more than a month after the murder no  one has been charged although officials  in truro promised to pursue every lead  the public was concerned about a hate  and that you know and again there's no  indication at this point that that's  that is the situation but those are  being followed through until they get  answers from police six in nova scotia  are living in fear especially young men  some of whom have told us they're afraid  to appear on camera they don't know what  to make of this murder whether they're  safe  many of them are afraid to go out it  gave a very  bad impression about nova scotia  internationally in in india especially  where six left so we slowly we explained  that this was just one incident police  questioned and released a man they call  a person of interest sources close to  their investigation say they're  currently waiting for the results of  forensic testing before making their  next move  an online fundraiser collected almost  thousand dollars in donations helping  return prabjot to his mother in india  where his body has been cremated  ross lord global news fero nova scotia  over a century on and still going strong  next a canadian veteran enjoys a life  well lived  as canadians gather around the dinner  table to give thanks this weekend an  ontario family is celebrating a life  well lived  sunday marks the 102nd birthday of a man  who defied the odds and gave back to his  country  morgan campbell explains  [Music]  as family friends and wartime reenactors  make their way past graham clap's sasha  home it is easy to see he has a lot to  be grateful for this thanksgiving  one viewer at a time you know when you  get a certain age we don't always get  the next number  looking dapper with a fresh new haircut  the world war ii veteran is celebrating  his 102nd birthday and after speaking to  his family it is easy to see why so many  people have gathered for him he has a  kind soul he is warm and loves a good  laugh he was in his late 90s still doing  planks every day so he is not about to  give up anytime soon he still walks he  gave up driving about a year ago and he  has  a lot of support from from the families  and from his neighbors  on his street  at 102 klapp still enjoys the comforts  of his own home with a live-in caregiver  and he only gave up his driver's license  last year born in south wales the  retired sergeant immigrated to canada 95  years ago in 1926 and later served as a  radio operator at stops across europe in  the canadian army during the second  world war he joined the first special  service force in 1942 nicknamed the  devil's brigade by the germans after 99  days of continuous action  serving alongside a mixture of canadians  and americans clapp was one of 3 600 men  who fought in italy and france today he  is one of only 30 remaining men who wore  the red spearhead of the fssf  still alive it's important to remember  and not to forget  um you know and and appreciate  the freedom the liberty the privileges  everything we have today as a result of  the courage that um these veterans um  have made on on behalf of not just us  you know in canada but the world  the parade is a small token of the  gratitude people have for a man who  inspires others to work hard do what's  right and give back he's just remarkable  that and he's a uh  he just sets the bar really high for the  rest of us to  you know keep on tracking and keep on  going and at 102 years old clap is  showing no signs of slowing down  we all live simple lives  morgan campbell global news  thankful to graham clapp and other  veterans like him and those on this new  front line against covet 19 during this  second pandemic thanksgiving i'm farah  nasser on behalf of our global news  family we are so grateful to you and  your family for not only giving us your  time but also trusting us to bring you  the news every night  tonight's your canada is a chinook arch  in calgary skies on a gorgeous autumn  day we'd love to see your corner of  canada so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  until tomorrow take care of yourselves  and each other happy thanksgiving  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

